BALA KANDA - Sarga 1
- Verse 38: At Nandigrāma, Bharata placed Rāma's sacred sandals (pādukās) upon the throne as the sovereign proxy (nyāsa) of Rāma, administering the kingdom purely as their custodian (Gita Press / Govindarāja).
- Verse 40: Rāma left Citrakūṭa and entered the Daṇḍaka forest because repeated visits from the citizens and royal family of Ayodhyā would disrupt the peace and penance of the local munis and r̥ṣis (Gita Press / Govindarāja).
- Verse 46: The condensed śloka states that Śūrpaṇakhā was disfigured by Rāma (tēna virūpitā); the full narrative in Araṇya Kāṇḍa explains that Lakṣmaṇa cut off her nose and ears on Rāma's command after she lunged to attack Sītā (Gita Press / Govindarāja).
- Verse 76: Although Śloka 75 only mentions capture in general terms (grahaṇaṁ samupāgamat), Indrajit had bound Hanumān using the Brahmāstra (astrēṇa). By Lord Brahmā's boon, the divine weapon released its hold the moment the Rākṣasas tied him with physical ropes (yantriṇaḥ). Hanumān willingly endured being bound so that he could be brought directly into Rāvaṇa's court (Gita Press / Govindarāja).

BALA KANDA - Sarga 2
- Verse 15: Classical commentators (Govindarāja and Tilaka) explain that the spontaneous verse "Mā niṣāda..." is simultaneously an esoteric benediction (maṅgala-śloka) addressed to Śrīman Nārāyaṇa (Mā-niṣāda: the abode of Goddess Lakṣmī) that contains the seed (bīja) of all seven Kāṇḍas of the Rāmāyaṇa:
  1. Bāla Kāṇḍa ("Mā-niṣāda"): The incarnation and wedding of Rāma with Sītā (Lakṣmī).
  2. Ayodhyā Kāṇḍa ("pratiṣṭhāṁ tvam agamaḥ"): Renouncing the established throne to depart into forest exile.
  3. Araṇya Kāṇḍa ("mithunāt ekam"): The tragic separation of the divine couple through Sītā's abduction.
  4. Kiṣkindhā Kāṇḍa ("mithunād ekam avadhīḥ"): Slaying one of the two brothers (Vāli of the Vāli-Sugrīva pair).
  5. Sundara Kāṇḍa ("kāma-mōhitam"): Discovering Sītā held in captivity by the lust-maddened Rāvaṇa.
  6. Yuddha Kāṇḍa ("avadhīḥ"): Slaying Rāvaṇa in the great battle and rescuing Sītā.
  7. Uttara Kāṇḍa ("śāśvatīḥ samāḥ pratiṣṭhām"): Establishing eternal righteous rule (Rāma-rājya) for thousands of years and attaining everlasting glory.
